  "content": "The user is asking about the Singleton design pattern in C#. I should explain what it is, why it's used, and provide a code example showing how to implement it in C#. I'll cover the concept, the implementation (thread-safe version), and briefly mention considerations/alternatives.\n</think>\n\n# Singleton Pattern in C#\n\nA **Singleton** is a design pattern that ensures a class has **exactly one instance** and provides a global point of access to it.\n\n## Why use it?\n\n- You need a single, shared instance (e.g., a logger, configuration manager, database connection pool).\n- You want to control access to that instance.\n\n## Basic Implementation\n\n```csharp\npublic class Singleton\n{\n    private static Singleton? _instance;\n\n    // Private constructor prevents external instantiation\n    private Singleton()\n    {\n    }\n\n    public static Singleton Instance\n    {\n        get\n        {\n            if (_instance == null)\n            {\n                _instance = new Singleton();\n            }\n            return _instance;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void DoWork()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(\"Singleton doing work\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Thread-Safe Version (recommended)\n\nThe basic version above is **not** thread-safe. Here's a safe implementation using `lock`:\n\n```csharp\npublic class Singleton\n{\n    private static Singleton? _instance;\n    private static readonly object _lock = new object();\n\n    private Singleton()\n    {\n    }\n\n    public static Singleton Instance\n    {\n        get\n        {\n            lock (_lock)\n            {\n                if (_instance == null)\n                {\n                    _instance = new Singleton();\n                }\n            }\n            return _instance;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Modern Alternative: Lazy Initialization with `Lazy<T>`\n\n```csharp\npublic class Singleton\n{\n    private static readonly Lazy<Singleton> _instance =\n        new Lazy<Singleton>(() => new Singleton());\n\n    private Singleton() { }\n\n    public static Singleton Instance => _instance.Value;\n}\n```\n\n`Lazy<T>` is thread-safe by default and is a clean, idiomatic approach in modern C#.\n\n## How to Use\n\n```csharp\nSingleton a = Singleton.Instance;\nSingleton b = Singleton.Instance;\n\nConsole.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(a, b)); // True — same instance\n```\n\n## Key Points\n\n| Aspect | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Private constructor** | Prevents `new Singleton()` from outside |\n| **Static property/field** | Holds the single instance |\n| **Thread safety** | Use `lock` or `Lazy<T>` |\n| **Serialization** | Implement `ISerializable` or use `GetObjectData` to return the singleton |\n\n## When to Avoid It\n\n- It can act as a **hidden global state**, making code harder to test.\n- In modern C#, **dependency injection** (e.g., via `IServiceCollection`) is often preferred over manual singletons, since the container manages the single instance for you.\n\n```csharp\n// DI approach\nservices.AddSingleton<IMyService, MyService>();\n```\n\nThis achieves the same \"one instance\" guarantee while keeping your code loosely coupled and testable.",
